::MCR_U06_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Unit 6 standards synthesis - MLA.MCR.GM.1 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A scale drawing uses 1 inch for 8 feet. What real distance is represented by 5 inches?
{
~A. 13 feet#This adds 8 and 5. Use the scale rate by multiplying.
=B. 40 feet#Correct. 5 x 8 \= 40 feet.
~C. 8 feet#This represents only 1 inch on the drawing.
~D. 5 feet#This ignores the scale rate.
}
::MCR_U06_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Unit 6 standards synthesis - MLA.MCR.GM.1 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (0,0), (6,0), (6,3), and (0,3). What is its area?
{
~A. 9 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 18 units#The number is correct, but area needs square units.
~C. 12 square units#This does not use the full rectangle dimensions. Area of a rectangle is length times width.
=D. 18 square units#Correct. Area \= 6 x 3 \= 18 square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Unit 6 standards synthesis - MLA.MCR.GM.1 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A prism is 5 ft by 4 ft by 2 ft. What is its volume?
{
=A. 40 cubic ft#Correct. Volume \= 5 x 4 x 2 \= 40 cubic ft.
~B. 11 cubic ft#This adds dimensions. Volume requires multiplication.
~C. 20 cubic ft#This uses only two dimensions.
~D. 40 square ft#The number is correct, but volume uses cubic units.
}
::MCR_U06_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Unit 6 standards synthesis - MLA.MCR.GM.1 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. What is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. 21#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. 108#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find c.
=C. 15#Correct. 9² + 12² \= 225, and sqrt(225) \= 15.
~D. 225#This is c², not c.
}
::MCR_U06_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Unit 6 standards synthesis - MLA.MCR.GM.1 through MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which final answer has the correct unit for density?
{
~A. 6 cubic cm#Cubic cm measures volume, not density.
=B. 6 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density compares mass to volume.
~C. 6 square cm#Square cm measures area.
~D. 6 cm#Centimeters measure length.
}
